(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-cytopenias-hemochromatosis-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cytopenias and Hemochromatosis Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which haptoglobin result is expected with intravascular hemolysis?","choices":["Elevated with jaundice","Decreased or absent","Markedly increased","Normal in every case"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In intravascular hemolysis, free hemoglobin released into the plasma is picked up by haptoglobin, which carries it to the liver, so haptoglobin is used up and becomes decreased or absent. The binding is not complete, which is why some hemoglobin also reaches the urine. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 66, 67"},{"q":"Which oral vitamin occasionally produces a response in sideroblastic anemia?","choices":["Vitamin B12","Pyridoxine","Folic acid","Vitamin C"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. There are occasional responses to oral pyridoxine in sideroblastic anemia. Treatment otherwise removes the offending agent if acquired, uses red cell transfusion when severe, and refers to hematology for diagnostic or transfusion support; recombinant erythropoietin typically does not help. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How does the course of sickle-hemoglobin C disease compare with sickle cell anemia and sickle cell trait?","choices":["Same as sickle cell anemia","Between the two","Worse than sickle cell anemia","Milder than sickle cell trait"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemoglobin C comes from a different beta globin mutation and does not polymerize. Sickle-hemoglobin C disease, the combination of one C and one S, generally has a course between sickle cell anemia and sickle cell trait. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of iron deficiency anemia in adults?","choices":["Low dietary iron","Gut malabsorption","Pregnancy demand","Chronic blood loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic blood loss, from the gastrointestinal tract or from menses, is the most common cause of iron deficiency anemia in adults. That is why treatment starts with finding and treating the source, such as a gastrointestinal bleed or heavy menstrual loss. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which population has the highest listed prevalence of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency?","choices":["Nigerians","Japanese and Koreans","Thai people","Kurdish Jews"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The prevalence list is led by Kurdish Jews at 60 to 70 percent, followed by Sardinians (4 to 35 percent by village), Nigerians (22 percent), Thai people (17 percent) and American Black people (11 to 12 percent). — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Macrocytic anemia is defined by a mean corpuscular volume above which value?","choices":["80 fL","100 fL","115 fL","125 fL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Macrocytic anemia is anemia with a mean corpuscular volume greater than 100 fL. The normal mean corpuscular volume is 80-100 fL, so anything above the top of that range is macrocytic, and the causes then split into megaloblastic and non-megaloblastic groups. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 7, 38"},{"q":"When is parenteral iron indicated for iron deficiency anemia?","choices":["At diagnosis, before any oral iron trial","As first-line therapy for menstrual loss","Whenever the hemoglobin is below normal","Intolerance or refractoriness to oral iron"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Parenteral iron is for patients who are intolerant of or refractory to oral iron preparations; it is given over 15 minutes to 1 hour. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which total iron-binding capacity result is expected in iron deficiency anemia?","choices":["Normal total iron-binding capacity","Variable total iron-binding capacity","Increased total iron-binding capacity","Decreased total iron-binding capacity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The iron panel of iron deficiency shows a reduced serum ferritin, reduced transferrin saturation and an increased total iron-binding capacity, with microcytic, hypochromic cells. Anemia of chronic disease has the opposite binding capacity, normal or low. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 15, 27"},{"q":"Which finding is often the first indication of hemolysis, even before anemia or symptoms appear?","choices":["Visible jaundice","High-output heart failure","Rising reticulocyte count","Falling reticulocyte count"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An unexpected increase in the reticulocyte count, even without anemia or symptoms, should prompt consideration of hemolysis, and it is often the first indication. The marrow is compensating for the shortened red cell life span. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 8, 61, 63, 70"},{"q":"During an acute attack of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, which peripheral smear findings may be seen?","choices":["Sickle-shaped red cells","Heinz bodies and bite cells","Ring sideroblasts","Hypersegmented neutrophils"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Without reduced glutathione the red cell is damaged by oxidative stress and Heinz bodies form inside it; splenic macrophages then remove them by taking a chunk out of the cell, leaving bite cells. Both may appear on the smear during an attack. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 77, 82"},{"q":"Which test is required to diagnose aplastic anemia?","choices":["Peripheral blood smear","Repeat complete blood count","Reticulocyte count","Bone marrow biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A bone marrow biopsy is required for the diagnosis of aplastic anemia and to exclude other causes of pancytopenia. Urgent or emergent consultation with a hematologist/oncologist is indicated, and the treatment is bone marrow transplant. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 8, 47, 58, 59"},{"q":"After secondary causes are ruled out, what is the initial treatment of autoimmune hemolytic anemia?","choices":["Hydroxyurea","Glucocorticoids","Splenectomy","Folic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of autoimmune hemolytic anemia is glucocorticoids initially, after ruling out secondary causes. Rituximab is the other named agent, with immunosuppressants and splenectomy as further options. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Besides cautious transfusion, which supportive measure is part of thalassemia treatment?","choices":["Oral iron","Glucocorticoids","Folic acid","Hydroxyurea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thalassemia treatment is supportive: transfusion when absolutely necessary (it can exacerbate iron overload), folic acid and genetic counseling. No disease-modifying drug is named for thalassemia. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 74, 87, 89"},{"q":"Which cause of iron deficiency acts through increased demand rather than loss or poor absorption?","choices":["Pregnancy","Celiac sprue","Heavy menses","Tea drinking"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Increased demand is its own category of iron deficiency: pregnancy and growth in infancy or adolescence. Blood loss, dietary factors and malabsorption are the other categories, so pregnancy is the one that fits demand. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"With which condition is acquired sideroblastic anemia usually associated?","choices":["Chronic blood loss","Myelodysplastic syndrome","An X-linked inherited defect","Chronic kidney disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sideroblastic anemia comes in three types: hereditary (usually X-linked), acquired (usually associated with myelodysplastic syndrome), and acquired reversible, from alcohol, drugs such as isoniazid or chlorambucil, copper deficiency, hypothermia or lead poisoning. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"In an elderly patient with a cytopenia, which causes does the lifespan table list to check?","choices":["Abnormal newborn screens","Pregnancy","Iron or B12 deficiency, drugs","Heavy menstrual bleeding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the elderly, check iron and B12 deficiency, chronic kidney disease, drugs and marrow disorders such as myelodysplastic syndrome, and tailor treatment to function, comorbidities and goals. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which iron panel result shows there is no iron overload?","choices":["Ferritin <300 and saturation >45%","Ferritin >300 and saturation <45%","Ferritin >300 and saturation >45%","Ferritin <300 and saturation <45%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If ferritin is less than 300 micrograms/L and the transferrin saturation is less than 45 percent, there is no iron overload. Both must be below their cut-offs, and because ferritin is an acute phase reactant the whole laboratory picture is read with caution. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"What is a defining feature of aplastic anemia?","choices":["Inappropriately low erythropoietin","Iron-restricted erythropoiesis","Loss of hematopoietic stem cells","Impaired deoxyribonucleic acid synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loss of hematopoietic stem cells is a defining feature of aplastic anemia, which shows as pancytopenia, loss of red cells, platelets and white cells. Drugs, chemicals, viruses or mutations can alter the stem cells and predispose them to autoimmune destruction. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 39, 53, 55, 56"},{"q":"Besides hemoglobin and iron, what is monitored in anemia of chronic kidney disease?","choices":["Blood pressure","Methylmalonic acid","Absolute neutrophil count","Haptoglobin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Follow-up of anemia of chronic kidney disease means monitoring hemoglobin, iron and blood pressure, with nephrology for advanced chronic kidney disease, erythropoiesis-stimulating agent decisions or uncertainty. The agent is considered by chronic kidney disease stage, hemoglobin and risk. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 47, 53, 59, 66, 106"},{"q":"Which is a common secondary cause of autoimmune hemolytic anemia?","choices":["Lymphoma","Fava beans","Copper poisoning","Chronic liver disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Common secondary causes are viruses (human immunodeficiency virus, hepatitis, Epstein-Barr virus), autoimmune disorders (lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, scleroderma, ulcerative colitis), lymphoproliferative disorders such as leukemia, lymphoma and multiple myeloma, and pregnancy. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 65, 71, 100"},{"q":"When reviewing a new anemia, which value shows whether the bone marrow response is adequate?","choices":["The reticulocyte index","The mean corpuscular volume","The red cell distribution width","The serum ferritin level"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The initial approach reviews the complete blood count across the three cell lines, evaluates for pancytopenia and the mean corpuscular volume, then reviews the reticulocyte index to decide whether the bone marrow response is adequate. Reticulocytes are the newly released red cells, so they measure marrow output. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the most frequent cause of B12 deficiency?","choices":["Increased demand","Drug effect","Pernicious anemia","Low dietary intake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pernicious anemia is the most frequent cause of B12 deficiency. It is an autoimmune reaction against parietal cells and/or intrinsic factor itself, so intrinsic factor is not available to carry B12 to the distal ileum, and it brings gastric atrophy and an increased risk of gastric cancer. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 42, 44"},{"q":"Why does the distribution of sickle cell disease follow the malaria belt?","choices":["Carriers resist severe malaria","Malaria causes the mutation","Carriers get malaria more","Antimalarials cause sickling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The distribution follows the malaria belt closely because heterozygous carriers of sickle cell trait are 90 percent less likely to experience severe malaria than people with only normal hemoglobin A, a survival advantage where malaria is common. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which oral iron schedule is described as probably best for absorption and side effects?","choices":["Dosing with each meal","Several doses each day","Bedtime dosing with milk","Alternate-day dosing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Recent studies show better absorption and fewer side effects with less frequent dosing, and alternate-day dosing is probably best, although adherence can be a problem. Oral iron otherwise has poor absorption, many side effects and poor adherence. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the leading cause of death for patients with sickle cell disease?","choices":["Splenic infarction","Leg ulcers","Priapism","Acute chest syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute chest syndrome is the leading cause of death in sickle cell disease, often associated with an acute pain event, with greater than 10 percent mortality in adults. Its cause is multifactorial: infection, infarction, pulmonary embolism and fat embolism. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"If B12 and folate levels are borderline but suspicion continues, which tests come next?","choices":["Homocysteine and methylmalonic acid","Ferritin and transferrin saturation","Bone marrow biopsy and smear","Haptoglobin and bilirubin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. B12 and folate levels are the initial tests for macrocytic anemia, with a peripheral smear, but they are not very sensitive. If they are borderline or clinical suspicion continues, homocysteine and methylmalonic acid levels are checked: both rise in B12 deficiency, homocysteine alone in folate deficiency. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 47, 50, 58, 67"},{"q":"What should accompany ferrous sulfate because of its most common gastrointestinal side effect?","choices":["An antacid for nausea","A proton pump inhibitor","An antidiarrheal agent","A laxative or stool softener"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal side effects of ferrous sulfate are common, nausea and especially constipation, so it is given with at least an as-needed laxative or stool softener. Side effects drive the poor adherence oral iron is known for. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which pattern of ferritin and total iron-binding capacity favors anemia of chronic disease over iron deficiency anemia?","choices":["Low ferritin, low binding capacity","Normal or high ferritin, high binding capacity","Normal or high ferritin, low binding capacity","Low ferritin, high binding capacity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anemia of chronic disease has a ferritin that is normal or increased and a total iron-binding capacity that is decreased (normal or low on the differential), while iron deficiency has a low ferritin and a raised binding capacity. These two values are where the comparisons agree. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slides 13, 27"},{"q":"Which iron panel pattern is expected in sideroblastic anemia?","choices":["High serum iron, saturation and ferritin","Low ferritin, high binding capacity","Normal or high ferritin, low binding","Low serum iron, saturation and ferritin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sideroblastic anemia carries an excess of total body iron, with increased serum iron, transferrin saturation and ferritin. That separates it from iron deficiency, another microcytic anemia in the differential, where all three fall. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"An absolute neutrophil count of 1,200 cells per microliter is classified as which degree of neutropenia?","choices":["Not neutropenic","Mild","Moderate","Severe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mild neutropenia is an absolute neutrophil count of 1,000-1,500 cells per microliter, so 1,200 is mild. Moderate is 500-999 and severe is below 500. — Cytopenias_Hemochromatosis_Objectives_Enhanced.pptx, Slide 59"}]};
